# Owner approval keys (claude-agents decision 0114 section 2, as amended by 0173), OpenSSH allowed_signers format:
#   owner@stigready namespaces="stigready-owner-approval",verify-required sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com AAAA... <comment>
# A line counts only when ALL of these hold (scripts/check-copy-vs-marketplace.py sk_signers, owner_enrolled):
#   - the principal is owner@stigready and the options name namespaces="stigready-owner-approval";
#   - the options carry verify-required and no cert-authority; the checker then requires the user-verification
#     flag (a PIN) on every approval signature, since ssh-keygen allowed_signers has no such option;
#   - the key type is FIDO2 (sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com or sk-ecdsa-sha2-nistp256@openssh.com);
#   - the owner's GitHub account lists the same key as an SSH signing key
#     (https://api.github.com/users/<OWNER_GITHUB_LOGIN>/ssh_signing_keys).
# The type alone proves nothing: software can make an sk- key and signature. What protects the gate is that only a key
# the owner enrolled on the owner's own GitHub account counts, and agents never sign in as the owner.
#
# The copy-removal-gate check reads this file from the BASE branch, by git object, never from the pull request. Any
# change to it (an edit, emptying it, deleting it, a symlink) is itself removal-class, needs a signature by a key that
# already counts, and must be the PR's only withdrawal. While no key counts, nothing can be approved.
#
# The first key (approvals/owner/README.md, "The first key"): the owner makes it and enrols it on GitHub; an agent's PR
# adds the public key line here. Nobody merges by hand.
